the recent say about rattling from  Beijing have made preparing air raid  shelters a key part of taiwan's civil  defense strategy but as DW's Jerry Chan  reports not everyone thinks those  shelters are adequate  these yellow signs are everywhere across  Taipei they show the locations and the  capacity of thousands of Airway shelters  where people can hide in case of an  attack  now a smartphone app has been launched  that shows a real-time map so users can  quickly find the nearest shelter  officials want residents to get familiar  with these spots in subway stations car  Parks underground shopping malls and  even basements of private properties  this is one of the many car parks in the  city that can also serve as an airway  shelter however we cannot see any signs  of water or emergency supplies  and that is a problem  efficacy groups say many of these  shelters are very poorly equipped  Taiwan is hot and humid and car Parks  don't have any air conditioning and  there aren't sanitary facilities it's  not suitable to serve as a long-term  shelter  leads the civil defense advocacy group  Malaysia Taiwan Association  his organization regularly hosts first  aid and self-defense classes for the  public  apart from the lack of facilities Chia  is worried about the reliable access to  these shelters  many basements in these residential  buildings are occupied they might be  used as storage space and they're not  properly maintained or could even be  blocked  but what will happen in the worst case  scenario if China attacks  an analyst at the government fund  Institute of National Defense and  security research told us these shelters  are different from what many people  expect  practices  they are supposed to provide temporary  protection when an airstrike occurs  missile attacks are usually over a very  short time  three to four minutes  they might also go on for a couple of  hours  so facilities such as ventilation or  drinking water supplies are usually not  part of these shelter designs  the most important thing is that people  know where the shelters are Chen said  but many Taiwanese people have grown  numb to beijing's repeated shows of  Falls over the years so now the  government not only has to maintain  security facilities it also faces the  challenge of keeping up people's  vigilance
 

